message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
title: 'Engineering Political Schelling Points: Disclosing a Common Source Removes Evidence but Leaves Coordination Unless the Disclosure Is Common Knowledge'
authors:
- name: PIATRA Institute
date-released: '2026-11-01'
url: https://piatra.institute/papers/engineering-political-schelling-points.pdf
repository-code: https://github.com/piatra-institute/papers-engineering-political-schelling-points
abstract: 'When many accounts amplify a call to collective action, the message matters as evidence that the action can succeed and as a shared reference that lets people predict one another. A regime-change global game separates the two: a message''s perceived precision depends on how independent its sources are believed to be, and a reach parameter measures whether its receipt is common knowledge. The equilibrium cutoff decomposes exactly into a closed-form evidential channel and a coordination channel equal to an explicit, reach-dependent multiplier on it; at baseline coordination is 80% of the effect when receipt is common knowledge. A message every receiver believes uninformative has no effect however visible, but a receiver who alone learns that the source is common keeps following it because others do. Disclosing a common source in isolation removes 11% of the effect of a campaign of 3 accounts at full reach; as common knowledge it removes 71%; when it also makes receipt common knowledge it raises participation in a mobilizing message, to first order, exactly when a publicity-credibility ratio exceeds one. If participation erodes the status quo, a temporary message persists only above a closed-form feedback threshold and only if it pushes participation past an unstable state. Exposure data cannot separate persuasion, coordination and the majority illusion; a laboratory design crossing visibility with incentives can, with 7 to 74 groups of 20 per cell depending on how fully participants act on coordination. All results are exact computations in a stylised model with illustrative parameters.'
